```html
<style>
  .feed { container-type: inline-size; }
  .card { display: grid; gap: 1rem; }
  .card img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }
  @container (min-width: 34rem) {
    .card { grid-template-columns: 10rem 1fr; }
  }
</style>
<section class="feed">
  <article class="card"><img src="cover.jpg" width="320" height="200" alt=""><div><h2>Guide</h2><p>Readable in any host.</p></div></article>
</section>
```
The card changes when its own container can support two columns, independent of the whole viewport.

```css
.profile { display: grid; gap: 1rem; }
.profile__actions { display: grid; gap: 0.5rem; }

@media (min-width: 40rem) {
  .profile { grid-template-columns: 12rem 1fr; }
  .profile__actions { display: flex; }
}
```
The base remains complete. The query changes relationships only after the component has room for them.

```css
.article {
  width: min(100% - 2rem, 70ch);
  margin-inline: auto;
}
.article h1 {
  font-size: clamp(2rem, 1.4rem + 3vw, 4rem);
}
.button {
  padding: 0.65em 1em;
  border: 1px solid;
}
```
The article follows available width and readable measure, the heading grows within limits, and button padding follows its own text size.
